Basic Syntax
Sql

Copy code
CREATE INDEX index_name ON table_name (column1 [ASC|DESC], column2 [ASC|DESC], ...);
Key points:

index_name → Unique name for the index in the schema.
table_name → Table on which the index is created.
Columns can be sorted in ascending (ASC) or descending (DESC) order.
By default, Db2 creates a B-tree index.
Example 1 – Single Column Index
Sql

Copy code
CREATE INDEX idx_employee_lastname ON employee (last_name ASC);
This creates an index on the last_name column of the employee table to speed up searches by last name.

Example 2 – Composite (Multi-Column) Index
Sql

Copy code
CREATE INDEX idx_employee_dept_salary ON employee (department_id ASC, salary DESC);
This index helps queries that filter or sort by department_id and salary.

Example 3 – Unique Index
Sql

Copy code
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_employee_email ON employee (email);
This ensures that the email column has unique values and improves lookup speed.

Example 4 – Index on Expression
Sql

Copy code
CREATE INDEX idx_upper_lastname ON employee (UPPER(last_name));
This is useful for case-insensitive searches.

Best Practices
Index only when needed – Too many indexes slow down INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE.
Use composite indexes wisely – Order columns based on query patterns.
Avoid indexing small tables – Full table scans may be faster.
Monitor index usage – Use EXPLAIN or Db2 monitoring tools.
Consider clustering indexes – For range queries and ordered retrieval.
